      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In medical treatment of angina pectoris the 3 major groups of antianginal agents are nitrates, beta blockers and calcium antagonists. Now a new class of drugs is introduced in the therapy: the potassium channel openers. One of the first potassium channel openers is nicorandil. We examined the acute hemodynamic effects of 2 different oral nicorandil doses in patients with coronary heart disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty patients with angiographic proven coronary heart disease and stable angina pectoris were treated with a dose of 2 x 10 mg Nicorandil on day 1 and 2 x 20 mg Nicorandil on day 2, while being hemodynamically monitored on an intensive care unit with a pulmonary artery catheter."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Through the hemodynamic monitoring a dose dependent, significant reduction of systolic blood pressure was found (6%/9%), while the heart rate increased dose dependently (6%/11%). The rate pressure product as a marker of myocardial oxygen demand did not increase significantly."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The oral application of nicorandil causes an acute, dose dependent, significant decrease in systolic blood pressure and induces a reflextachycardia."
        }
      ]},
